review: W-027 — container lifecycle: prune command (round 2)
sella: qa-lead
command: independent re-review on Opus 5 (round 1 ran on a sub-tier model).
  Read brief, opera front matter, prune.ts, prune.test.ts, main.ts wiring,
  shim/worktree.ts reclaimWorktrees. Ran: check studio --repo . --json;
  npm run -s typecheck; npx eslint prune.ts; prune.test.ts; one fresh
  mutation on the merged-branch guard; four end-to-end runPrune runs against
  throwaway git fixtures.
verdict: failed

Round 1's findings, verified:

1. [block] opus.red_evidence — CONFIRMED. `check studio --repo . --json`
   returns level "block" for opera/W-027.md: "missing red log(s) for
   behaviour(s) 1, 2, 3, 4, 5". `studio/ci/reds/W-027/` does not exist
   (reds exist for W-020, W-021, W-024, W-025, W-028, W-029 — so the
   convention was followed elsewhere in the same cascade). Standing rule
   "Test-first with a recorded red"; evidence is produced, never backfilled.
   Nothing can retroactively create these; the opus needs its reds re-run
   against a reverted implementation, or the gap acknowledged as a lesson.

2. [coverage gap] behaviour 5 untested — CONFIRMED, and exercised by hand to
   establish whether a defect hides behind it. runPrune has no test anywhere
   in the repo; `main.ts:135` is its only caller. Manual end-to-end runs
   against throwaway fixtures (git init, studio/opera with done/building
   opera, opus/* branches, a live .bisellium/worktrees slot):

     A  prune --studio <fix>/studio --repo <fix>
        -> "branch removed: opus/W-100 (done and merged)", exit 0;
           opus/W-100 gone, opus/W-101 (building) kept.          CORRECT
     B  prune --studio <fix>/nope --repo <fix>
        -> "no opera directory at <fix>/nope", exit 2.           CORRECT
     C  prune (no flags, cwd=repo root)
        -> "no opera directory at <repo>", exit 2.               CORRECT
     D  prune --studio <fix>/studio --repo <fix>/not-a-repo
        -> "nothing to prune", exit 0.                           see 6
     E  prune --studio=<fix>/studio --repo <fix>
        -> falls back to cwd, exit 2.                            see 5
     F  prune --help
        -> falls back to cwd, exit 2, no usage printed.          see 5
     G  live worktree (.bisellium/worktrees/slot1, merged, clean) + done
        merged opus branch
        -> "worktree removed: <...>/slot1 (merged)" AND
           "branch removed: opus/W-300 (done and merged)", exit 0. CORRECT

   Conclusion: the untested behaviour 5 is a coverage gap, not a latent
   functional defect. The happy path, the combined worktree+branch report
   and the exit-2 path all behave as the brief specifies. This does not make
   the gap acceptable — nothing in the repo stops the next edit from
   breaking it silently — but it is not the reason this round fails.

3. [minor] dead code — CONFIRMED by eslint (2 warnings, exit 0):
   `readdirSync` imported unused (prune.ts:2), `PRUNE_USAGE` assigned unused
   (prune.ts:77). Consequence beyond tidiness: `prune` has no usage path at
   all. Unlike close.ts (which prints CLOSE_USAGE when its positional is
   missing), prune has no required positional, so PRUNE_USAGE is unreachable
   and `--help` / `--studio=<x>` / any typo silently degrade to studio=cwd
   (cases E, F). Harmless from the repo root; from inside a studio-shaped cwd
   it would run a real prune the operator did not ask for.

New in round 2:

4. [block] THE SUITE DOES NOT BITE ON THE SAFETY GUARD. Round 1 ran one
   mutation (the `state !== "done"` check) and it was caught. A second,
   harder mutation on the clause the brief calls the safety property
   ("Safe: only merged+done branches are deleted") SURVIVES:

     prune.ts:61   if (!mergedSet.has(branch)) {   ->   if (false) {

   Run against a copy (packages/cli/src/prune.mut.ts + prune.mut.test.ts,
   deleted after the run; tracked sources untouched): 4 tests, 4 pass, 0 fail.
   Test 3 ("keeps an unmerged branch even if opus is done") does not catch it
   because git's own `branch -d` refuses to delete an unmerged branch, so the
   branch still lands in kept[] — with reason "delete failed" instead of
   "not merged into HEAD". The test asserts only `removed.length === 0` and
   that the branch appears in kept[], never the reason, so it passes for the
   wrong reason. As written, the merged check in prune.ts is untested
   guesswork sitting on top of git's refusal; delete it and every test stays
   green. Asserting `kept[0].reason` in tests 2 and 3 closes this.

5. [advise] probatio.certifies.stale — the tests/lint/types gates certify
   tree:5ab03a0b4156..., the current source tree is tree:1752b55143. The
   certificates were legitimate when taken (round 1 was right about that);
   unrelated source churn since has aged them out. They must be re-run before
   any pass verdict, since a pass would be certifying a tree no gate covers.

6. [advise] silent success on a bad --repo. Case D above: a --repo that is
   not a git repository produces "nothing to prune" and exit 0. `git branch
   --list` fails, status !== 0, and pruneStaleOpusBranches returns an empty
   result indistinguishable from "clean repo". An operator who mistypes
   --repo is told the tree is clean. One `status !== 0` -> exit 1 in runPrune
   would fix it. Advisory, not blocking: close.ts does not validate --repo
   either, so this is house-wide, not a W-027 regression.

7. [advise] kept[] is computed and thrown away. runPrune reports only
   removals; every kept entry and its reason ("not merged into HEAD", "opus
   state is building", "no opus file found") is discarded, and the operator
   sees "nothing to prune" even when branches were deliberately spared. The
   brief's behaviour 5 says "reports both worktree and branch results" —
   satisfied on the removal side, but the diagnostic half of the data
   structure has no consumer.

Process record:

8. Round 1 disclosed that its first `review` invocation filed a real gate
   citing briefs/W-027.md as evidence. studio/opera/W-027.md now correctly
   cites ci/W-027-review-1.log — the front matter is clean. The append-only
   ledger is not: studio/.bisellium/events.jsonl lines 275 and 276 both carry
   workflow.gate.evidence = "briefs/W-027.md" for the failed review gate.
   That is permanent and must not be hand-edited; recording it here so the
   retro can file it under the evidence-backfilled-or-absent class.

9. The checkout moved under this review: git status was clean at the start
   and showed modifications to apps/web/src/screens/Inbox.tsx,
   packages/cli/src/rules/paths.ts, studio/opera/W-029.md and three untracked
   W-029 ci logs by the end — none of them mine (my two mutation copies were
   created and deleted under packages/cli/src/). W-027's own files did not
   move, so the verdict stands, but a reviewer sharing a checkout with a live
   builder is the no-builder-isolation hazard from retro 4b, still open.

Verified green, for the record:
- `npm run -s typecheck` exit 0.
- `node --import tsx --test packages/cli/src/prune.test.ts` — 4 pass, 0 fail.
- `npx eslint packages/cli/src/prune.ts` exit 0 (2 warnings, see 3).
- `check studio --repo .` exit 1, with the two blocking findings above
  (opus.red_evidence, traditio.present — the latter is the missing handoff
  for a building item, not a code defect).

Verdict: fail. Blocking: (1) no reds for any of the 5 behaviours, and (4) the
suite does not bite on the merged-branch safety guard. To pass: record the
reds honestly for a re-implementation rather than backfilling them, assert
kept[] reasons so the merged check is actually pinned, add a runPrune test
(behaviour 5), drop the dead import and constant, and re-run tests/lint/types
so the certificates name the current tree.
